 * cite91†Contributing L208:   * cite91†Apache-2.0 license L209: \nL210: More items\nL211: \nL212: # KernelBlaster\nL213: ## Project Intro\nL214: \nL215: Introducing KernelBlaster, a Memory-Augmented In-context Reinforcement Learning (MAIC-RL) framework\nL216: \nL217: Optimizing CUDA code across multiple GPU generations is difficult because the best implementation depends on a large and hardware-specific search space. A kernel that looks reasonable on one GPU can leave performance on the table on another, and simple rewrites are rarely enough to reach the best result.\nL218: Traditional compiler pipelines are limited by fixed heuristics, while fully finetuning large language models for every optimization setting is expensive. Many agentic CUDA workflows also have a simpler problem: they do not remember enough from previous exploration. That leads to repeated mistakes, biased sampling, and weaker optimization choices.\nL219: KernelBlaster is built to make that search smarter. Instead of treating each kernel as an isolated prompt, it combines profiling feedback, a persistent CUDA optimization knowledge base, and reinforcement-learning-style exploration. The agent does not just generate code; it profiles, reflects, retrieves prior optimization knowledge, explores new candidates, and updates its strategy over time.\nL220: The result is a reusable open-source framework for CUDA optimization with verification, profiling, replay, and reproducible evaluation built in.\nL221: \nL222: Compared to the PyTorch baseline, KernelBlaster achieves geometric mean speedups of 1.43x on KernelBench Level 1, 2.50x on Level 2, and 1.50x on Level 3.\nL223: ## Paper Link\nL224: \nL225: arXiv: cite92†arXiv:2602.14293†arxiv.org | PDF: cite93†KernelBlaster.pdf L226: ## Why KernelBlaster\nL227: Others  | KernelBlaster\nL228: --- | ---\nL229: CUDA optimization is hardware-agnostic and requires searching a large design space.  | KernelBlaster narrows that search with hardware-aware profiling-guided state extraction and targeted optimization selection.\nL230: Fixed compiler heuristics cannot easily adapt to every kernel or GPU generation.  | KernelBlaster adapts optimization decisions to each kernel and GPU generation through retrieval and iterative search.\nL231: Finetuning LLMs for optimization is costly and slow to iterate on.  | KernelBlaster improves optimization through in-context memory and RL-style exploration without depending on expensive task-specific finetuning.\nL232: Naive agent loops forget what they learned from earlier kernels and earlier rollouts.  | KernelBlaster keeps memory in the loop through a persistent optimization database and replay-driven exploration.\nL233: ## How It Works\nL234: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - intuit-ai-research/REMem · GitHub (https://github.com/intuit-ai-research/REMem)\nciteturn31906view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/intuit-ai-research/REMem\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 411\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - ybwang119/ASCL: [ICML 2026] Official code for ICML 2026 paper: Mitigating the Safety–Utility Trade-off in LLM Alignment via Adaptive Safe Context Learning · GitHub (https://github.com/ybwang119/ASCL)\nciteturn31906view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/ybwang119/ASCL\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 289\n"
